  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA subtle shortwave will lift into southern and eastern portions of \nthe forecast area this afternoon into this evening with enough \nascent and instability for isolated to scattered showers/storms, \nbest chances (30-50%) along and south of the US 24 corridor. Lower \nchances (20%) to the northwest along any outflow/differential \nheating boundaries. Flow and lift through the column remain weak \nwith locally heavy rainfall and a few lightning strikes potential \nhazards.\n\nBroad upper trough axis will swing east through the Great Lakes \nlater tonight into Saturday. This will force an upstream cold front \nsoutheast through the area later Saturday morning into Saturday \nafternoon. Overall moisture quality and frontal forcing appears weak \nwith only a low chance PoP (20%) for a brief shower/storm with the \nfrontal passage and/or a leading pre-frontal feature. Drier air does \nbriefly make inroads then into Saturday night and Sunday morning \npost-frontal with sfc dewpoints falling into the mid 50s to low 60s. \n\nA more active period of monitoring convective complexes riding west-\nnorthwest flow aloft remains in play later Sunday through the middle \nof next week. The initial theta-e surge in advance of a returning \nwarm front Sunday afternoon into Sunday night brings decent chances \nfor convection, though capping and uncertainty on if a MCS dropping \nsoutheast through IL/MO delays the moisture return with PoPs limited \nin the chance to likely category (40-70%) for now. A quasi-zonal \nfrontal boundary then lingers in the general vicinity Monday through \nWednesday on the northern fringes of a southern CONUS subtropical \nridge with several rounds of rain and storms possible. This pattern \nhas the potential to yield some hydro issues, along with a threat \nfor strong to severe storms, particularly Monday afternoon into \nMonday night.",
